# 18. FIREBASE AND SECURITY — CRITICAL

This is a production SaaS system. Security is mandatory.

Inspect and fix all existing Firestore and Storage rules.

## Firestore security

- Public users must never read private shop documents.
- Separate public storefront data from private shop data.
- Validate authentication.
- Validate shop membership.
- Validate role and permissions.
- Validate tenant ID.
- Restrict sensitive fields.
- Protect subscriptions.
- Protect payments.
- Protect audit logs.
- Protect staff records.
- Protect customers and orders.
- Prevent unauthorized updates to shop status and subscription.
- Do not rely on frontend hiding.

## Storage security

- Every upload must validate shop membership.
- Every read must follow the relevant privacy policy.
- Every delete must validate shop ownership or authorized staff permission.
- Payment proofs must be private.
- Prevent cross-tenant file access.
- Enforce allowed MIME types and maximum file sizes.

## Backend security

Use Firebase Admin SDK on a trusted server environment for privileged operations.

Never expose service account credentials or private secrets in frontend code.

Implement secure server-side operations for:

- Shop creation
- Payment approval
- Subscription activation
- Shop suspension
- Subscription extension
- Sensitive shop updates
- Secure stock transactions
- Critical order and billing operations

Validate every request on the server.

Use idempotency protection for payment approvals, billing and order creation.

Apply rate limiting to authentication-sensitive and public checkout endpoints.

Validate and sanitize user input.

Add audit logging for sensitive admin actions.

Never trust a shopId supplied by the browser without verifying membership.

If the existing project uses Firebase client-side writes for privileged operations, migrate those operations to secure backend endpoints.

